-- ccc29-xxviii-5-appeal-829.l4 -- Criminal Code (Canada), PART XXVII: sections 829 to 838 (summary appeal on transcript or agreed statement of facts),
-- 839 (appeals to the Court of Appeal) and 840 (fees and allowances). Layer 1: the law, objectively. The text is a plain-text rendering,
-- made by a converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30); it is not the official text.
-- The conventions of ccc29-xxviii-0-peace-810.l4 apply unchanged. The place type and its constructors come from ccc29-xxviii-4-appeal-812.l4.

§ `Section 829 -- Definition of appeal court (summary appeal on transcript)`

-- 829(1) Subject to subsection (2), for the purposes of sections 830 to 838, appeal court means, in any province, the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province. (2) If the appeal is from a conviction, judgment, verdict or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court consisting of a judge of the Nunavut Court of Justice, appeal court means a judge of the Court of Appeal of Nunavut.
-- src:17250 | Definition of appeal court
-- src:17251 | 829 (1) Subject to subsection (2), for the purposes of sections 830 to 838, appeal court means, in any province, the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province.
-- src:17252 | Nunavut
-- src:17253 | (2) If the appeal is from a conviction, judgment, verdict or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court consisting of a judge of the Nunavut Court of Justice, appeal court means a judge of the Court of Appeal of Nunavut.
@ref Criminal Code s 829(1) to s 829(2) (src:17250-17253)
DECLARE `s 829 appeal court` IS ONE OF
    `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
    `s 829(2) a judge of the Court of Appeal of Nunavut`

@ref Criminal Code s 829(1) to s 829(2) (src:17250-17253)
GIVEN place IS A `s 812(1) province or territory`
      `the appeal is from a conviction, judgment, verdict or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court consisting of a judge of the Nunavut Court of Justice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE `s 829 appeal court`
`s 829 the appeal court for sections 830 to 838` MEANS
    IF `the appeal is from a conviction, judgment, verdict or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court consisting of a judge of the Nunavut Court of Justice`
    THEN JUST `s 829(2) a judge of the Court of Appeal of Nunavut`
    ELSE CONSIDER place
         WHEN `s 812(1) Yukon` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) speaks of "any province"; Yukon is a territory
         WHEN `s 812(1) the Northwest Territories` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) speaks of "any province"; the Northwest Territories is a territory
         WHEN `s 812(1) Nunavut` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (1) speaks of "any province"; Nunavut is a territory, and subsection (2) does not apply
         WHEN `s 812(1) Ontario` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) Quebec` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) Nova Scotia` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) British Columbia` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) Prince Edward Island` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) Newfoundland and Labrador` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) New Brunswick` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) Manitoba` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) Saskatchewan` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`
         WHEN `s 812(1) Alberta` THEN JUST `s 829(1) the superior court of criminal jurisdiction for the province`

§ `Section 830 -- Appeals`

-- 830(1) A party to proceedings to which this Part applies or the Attorney General may appeal against a conviction, judgment, verdict of acquittal or verdict of not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder or of unfit to stand trial or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court on the ground that (a) it is erroneous in point of law; (b) it is in excess of jurisdiction; or (c) it constitutes a refusal or failure to exercise jurisdiction.
-- src:17254 | Appeals
-- src:17255 | 830 (1) A party to proceedings to which this Part applies or the Attorney General may appeal against a conviction, judgment, verdict of acquittal or verdict of not criminally responsible on account of mental disorder or of unfit to stand trial or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court on the ground that
-- src:17256 | (a) it is erroneous in point of law;
-- src:17257 | (b) it is in excess of jurisdiction; or
-- src:17258 | (c) it constitutes a refusal or failure to exercise jurisdiction.
@ref Criminal Code s 830(1) to s 830(1)(c) (src:17254-17258)
DECLARE `s 830(1) ground` IS ONE OF
    `s 830(1)(a) it is erroneous in point of law`
    `s 830(1)(b) it is in excess of jurisdiction`
    `s 830(1)(c) it constitutes a refusal or failure to exercise jurisdiction`
    `s 830(1) another ground`

@ref Criminal Code s 830(1) (src:17254-17255)
-- @name-test: no JUST FALSE branch; named for a power, answers NOTHING outside the person, the decision and the three grounds
GIVEN `the person is a party to proceedings to which this Part applies or the Attorney General` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the decision is a conviction, judgment, verdict of acquittal, verdict of not criminally responsible or of unfit to stand trial, or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court` IS A BOOLEAN
      ground IS A `s 830(1) ground`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 830(1) the person may appeal against the decision on the ground` MEANS
    IF `the person is a party to proceedings to which this Part applies or the Attorney General` AND `the decision is a conviction, judgment, verdict of acquittal, verdict of not criminally responsible or of unfit to stand trial, or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court`
    THEN CONSIDER ground
         WHEN `s 830(1)(a) it is erroneous in point of law` THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN `s 830(1)(b) it is in excess of jurisdiction` THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN `s 830(1)(c) it constitutes a refusal or failure to exercise jurisdiction` THEN JUST TRUE
         WHEN `s 830(1) another ground` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "on the ground that (a) ...; (b) ...; or (c) ..."
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A party to proceedings to which this Part applies or the Attorney General may appeal against a conviction, judgment, verdict ... or other final order or determination of a summary conviction court"

-- 830(2) An appeal under this section shall be based on a transcript of the proceedings appealed from unless the appellant files with the appeal court, within fifteen days of the filing of the notice of appeal, a statement of facts agreed to in writing by the respondent.
-- src:17259 | Form of appeal
-- src:17260 | (2) An appeal under this section shall be based on a transcript of the proceedings appealed from unless the appellant files with the appeal court, within fifteen days of the filing of the notice of appeal, a statement of facts agreed to in writing by the respondent.
@ref Criminal Code s 830(2) (src:17259-17260)
GIVEN `the appellant files with the appeal court a statement of facts` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days between the filing of the notice of appeal and the filing of the statement` IS A NUMBER
      `the statement of facts is agreed to in writing by the respondent` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 830(2) the appeal is required to be based on a transcript of the proceedings appealed from` MEANS
    IF `the appellant files with the appeal court a statement of facts` AND `the days between the filing of the notice of appeal and the filing of the statement` AT MOST 15 AND `the statement of facts is agreed to in writing by the respondent`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "unless the appellant files with the appeal court, within fifteen days of the filing of the notice of appeal, a statement of facts agreed to in writing by the respondent"
    ELSE JUST TRUE

-- 830(3) An appeal under this section shall be made within the period and in the manner directed by any applicable rules of court and where there are no such rules otherwise providing, a notice of appeal in writing shall be served on the respondent and a copy thereof, together with proof of service, shall be filed with the appeal court within thirty days after the date of the conviction, judgment or verdict of acquittal or other final order or determination that is the subject of the appeal.
-- src:17261 | Rules for appeals
-- src:17262 | (3) An appeal under this section shall be made within the period and in the manner directed by any applicable rules of court and where there are no such rules otherwise providing, a notice of appeal in writing shall be served on the respondent and a copy thereof, together with proof of service, shall be filed with the appeal court within thirty days after the date of the conviction, judgment or verdict of acquittal or other final order or determination that is the subject of the appeal.
@ref Criminal Code s 830(3) (src:17261-17262)
GIVEN `there are applicable rules of court otherwise providing` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the days after the date of the decision on which the notice of appeal is served and filed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 830(3) the notice of appeal is served and filed within thirty days after the date of the decision` MEANS
    IF `there are applicable rules of court otherwise providing`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "within the period and in the manner directed by any applicable rules of court"
    ELSE IF `the days after the date of the decision on which the notice of appeal is served and filed` AT MOST 30
         THEN JUST TRUE
         ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "within thirty days after the date of the conviction, judgment or verdict of acquittal or other final order or determination"

@ref Criminal Code s 830(3) (src:17261-17262)
GIVEN `the date of the decision that is the subject of the appeal` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 830(3) the last day for serving and filing the notice of appeal` MEANS
    `the date of the decision that is the subject of the appeal` PLUS 30

-- 830(4) The Attorney General of Canada has the same rights of appeal in proceedings instituted at the instance of the Government of Canada and conducted by or on behalf of that Government as the Attorney General of a province has under this section.
